<!DOCTYPE html><html xmlns:o="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:office" xmlns:w="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:word" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/TR/REC-html40"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><title>Conservation Biology</title></head><body><h1>conservation-biology</h1><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li>Biodiversity and Conservation</li><li>Conservation Biology â€” Biodiversity and Protected Areas</li><li>National Parks, Wildlife Reserves, Conservation Areas, Biodiversity Hotspots, Wetlands and Ramsar Sites</li><li>Protected Areas, Biodiversity Hotspots and Endangered Species</li><li>Protected Areas and Wildlife</li><li>Wildlife: Importance, Causes of Extinction and Conservation Strategies</li><li>IUCN Categories of Threatened Species</li><li>Endangered Species in Nepal</li></ul><h2>Biodiversity and Conservation</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Biodiversity: the variety of life at three levels — genetic, species, and ecosystem diversity.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Genetic diversity: variation in genes within a species; important for adaptation and survival.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Species diversity: number and abundance of species in an area; higher in tropics.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Ecosystem diversity: variety of habitats, biotic communities, and ecological processes in a region.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Biodiversity conservation: protecting and managing biological diversity; in-situ (in natural habitat) and ex-situ (outside habitat).</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>In-situ conservation: protected areas (national parks, wildlife reserves, conservation areas), community forests, sacred groves.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Ex-situ conservation: zoos, botanical gardens, seed banks, gene banks, cryopreservation.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Why conserve? Food, medicine, industry, ecological services (pollination, water, climate), aesthetic, ethical, and intrinsic value.</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Threats: habitat loss/fragmentation, overexploitation, pollution, invasive species, climate change, illegal trade (poaching).</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Nepal: global biodiversity hotspot (Himalaya); 64% of forests; community forestry (22,000+ groups manage ~36% of forest land); national parks and conservation areas.</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>NEB focus: concept of biodiversity, its conservation, and in-situ vs ex-situ strategies.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Biodiversity: genetic, species, ecosystem diversity.</td></tr><tr><td>Conservation: in-situ (natural habitat) + ex-situ (outside).</td></tr><tr><td>In-situ: protected areas, community forests, sacred groves.</td></tr><tr><td>Ex-situ: zoos, botanical gardens, seed banks, gene banks.</td></tr><tr><td>Threats: habitat loss, overexploitation, pollution, invasive species, climate change, poaching.</td></tr><tr><td>Nepal: Himalaya hotspot; 64% forest; community forestry (~36%).</td></tr><tr><td>Why conserve: food, medicine, ecology, aesthetics, ethics.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>Biodiversity has THREE levels: genetic, species, ecosystem (not just "number of species").</td></tr><tr><td>In-situ conservation protects species IN their natural habitat; ex-situ conserves them OUTSIDE (zoos, seed banks).</td></tr><tr><td>Protected areas (in-situ) are the most effective large-scale conservation strategy.</td></tr><tr><td>Community forestry (in-situ) gives local communities rights and responsibility; it is Nepal's success story.</td></tr><tr><td>A biodiversity HOTSPOT has high endemic species AND severe habitat loss (both criteria).</td></tr><tr><td>Conservation is not just "protecting animals"; it includes genetic, species, and ecosystem diversity.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Biodiversity is the variety of life at three levels — genetic, species, and ecosystem. Conservation biology protects it through in-situ strategies (protected areas, community forests, sacred groves) and ex-situ strategies (zoos, botanical gardens, seed banks, gene banks). Threats include habitat loss, overexploitation, pollution, invasive species, climate change, and poaching. Nepal, a Himalayan biodiversity hotspot with ~64% forest cover, leads globally in community forestry (22,000+ groups manage ~36% of forest land). NEB focuses on the concept of biodiversity, its conservation, and in-situ vs ex-situ strategies.</p><hr><h2>Conservation Biology â€” Biodiversity and Protected Areas</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Biodiversity: the variety of life at three levels — genetic, species, and ecosystem diversity.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Genetic diversity: variation in genes within a species; important for adaptation and survival.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Species diversity: number and abundance of species in an area; higher in tropics.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Ecosystem diversity: variety of habitats, biotic communities, and ecological processes in a region.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Biodiversity conservation: protecting and managing biological diversity; in-situ (in natural habitat) and ex-situ (outside habitat).</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>In-situ conservation: protected areas (national parks, wildlife reserves, conservation areas), community forests, sacred groves.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Ex-situ conservation: zoos, botanical gardens, seed banks, gene banks, cryopreservation.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Why conserve? Food, medicine, industry, ecological services (pollination, water, climate), aesthetic, ethical, and intrinsic value.</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Threats: habitat loss/fragmentation, overexploitation, pollution, invasive species, climate change, illegal trade (poaching).</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Nepal: global biodiversity hotspot (Himalaya); 64% of forests; community forestry (22,000+ groups manage ~36% of forest land); national parks and conservation areas.</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>NEB focus: concept of biodiversity, its conservation, and in-situ vs ex-situ strategies.</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>Biodiversity conservation involves safeguarding and scientifically managing diverse life forms to sustain their levels and provide lasting benefits for current and future generations.</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>It protects plant, animal, microbial, and genetic resources crucial for food production, agriculture, and ecosystem functions like soil fertilization, nutrient recycling, pest control, erosion prevention, and crop and tree pollination.</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>Conservation practices aim to manage nature and resources for present and future generations, ensuring the sustained well-being of humanity.</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>There are two basic strategies of biodiversity conservation: a.</td></tr><tr><td>16</td><td>In-Situ Conservation (within natural habitat): This method protects organisms in their natural habitats like national parks and other protected areas.</td></tr><tr><td>17</td><td>For instance, in Chitwan National Park, we have centers for Elephant Breeding, Gharial Conservation, and Vulture Conservation.</td></tr><tr><td>18</td><td>Ex-Situ Conservation (outside natural habitat): This type of conservation strategy includes protection of animals outside their natural habitats such as botanical or zoological gardens, cryopreservation, seed gene banks etc.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Biodiversity: genetic, species, ecosystem diversity.</td></tr><tr><td>Conservation: in-situ (natural habitat) + ex-situ (outside).</td></tr><tr><td>In-situ: protected areas, community forests, sacred groves.</td></tr><tr><td>Ex-situ: zoos, botanical gardens, seed banks, gene banks.</td></tr><tr><td>Threats: habitat loss, overexploitation, pollution, invasive species, climate change, poaching.</td></tr><tr><td>Nepal: Himalaya hotspot; 64% forest; community forestry (~36%).</td></tr><tr><td>Why conserve: food, medicine, ecology, aesthetics, ethics.</td></tr><tr><td>Biodiversity conservation involves safeguarding and scientifically managing diverse life forms to sustain their levels and provide lasting benefits for current and future generations.</td></tr><tr><td>It protects plant, animal, microbial, and genetic resources crucial for food production, agriculture, and ecosystem functions like soil fertilization, nutrient recycling, pest control, erosion prevention, and crop and tree pollination.</td></tr><tr><td>Conservation practices aim to manage nature and resources for present and future generations, ensuring the sustained well-being of humanity.</td></tr><tr><td>There are two basic strategies of biodiversity conservation: a.</td></tr><tr><td>In-Situ Conservation (within natural habitat): This method protects organisms in their natural habitats like national parks and other protected areas.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>Biodiversity has THREE levels: genetic, species, ecosystem (not just "number of species").</td></tr><tr><td>In-situ conservation protects species IN their natural habitat; ex-situ conserves them OUTSIDE (zoos, seed banks).</td></tr><tr><td>Protected areas (in-situ) are the most effective large-scale conservation strategy.</td></tr><tr><td>Community forestry (in-situ) gives local communities rights and responsibility; it is Nepal's success story.</td></tr><tr><td>A biodiversity HOTSPOT has high endemic species AND severe habitat loss (both criteria).</td></tr><tr><td>Conservation is not just "protecting animals"; it includes genetic, species, and ecosystem diversity.</td></tr><tr><td>Distinguish between key concepts in Biodiversity and Conservation.</td></tr><tr><td>Understand the differences between similar topics.</td></tr><tr><td>Practice identifying the correct application of principles.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Biodiversity is the variety of life at three levels — genetic, species, and ecosystem. Conservation biology protects it through in-situ strategies (protected areas, community forests, sacred groves) and ex-situ strategies (zoos, botanical gardens, seed banks, gene banks). Threats include habitat loss, overexploitation, pollution, invasive species, climate change, and poaching. Nepal, a Himalayan biodiversity hotspot with ~64% forest cover, leads globally in community forestry (22,000+ groups manage ~36% of forest land). NEB focuses on the concept of biodiversity, its conservation, and in-situ vs ex-situ strategies. Biodiversity conservation involves safeguarding and scientifically managing diverse life forms
to sustain their levels and provide lasting benefits for current and future generations. It protects
plant,  animal,  microbial,  and genetic resources  crucial  for food production, agriculture,  and
ecosystem functions like soil fertilization, nutrient recycling,  pest control, erosion  prevention,
and crop and tree pollination.  Conservation practices aim  to manage nature and resources  for
pr</p><hr><h2>National Parks, Wildlife Reserves, Conservation Areas, Biodiversity Hotspots, Wetlands and Ramsar Sites</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>National parks are legally protected areas established to conserve wildlife, biodiversity, and natural ecosystems, with strict restrictions on human activities such as hunting, logging, and settlement.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Wildlife reserves are protected areas focused primarily on the conservation of particular species or groups of wildlife, allowing limited sustainable use by local communities.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Conservation areas are regions where conservation of biodiversity is integrated with sustainable development and community participation, balancing ecological protection with human livelihoods.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Biodiversity hotspots are regions with exceptionally high species richness and endemism that are under severe threat from human activities; they cover only 2.3% of Earth's surface but harbor over 50% of endemic plant species.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Wetlands are ecosystems saturated with water, either permanently or seasonally, supporting rich biodiversity and providing critical services like water purification, flood control, and carbon storage.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Ramsar sites are wetlands of international importance designated under the Ramsar Convention (1971) for their ecological, botanical, zoological, limnological, or hydrological significance.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Nepal has 12 national parks, 1 wildlife reserve, 6 conservation areas, and 10 Ramsar sites, reflecting its commitment to in-situ conservation.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>In-situ conservation protects species within their natural habitats (national parks, reserves), while ex-situ conservation protects species outside natural habitats (zoos, botanical gardens, seed banks).</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Buffer zones around protected areas allow sustainable resource use by local communities, reducing pressure on core conservation zones.</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Protected area management involves zoning, anti-poaching patrols, habitat restoration, community engagement, and monitoring of species populations.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>National parks: strict protection, no resource extraction</td></tr><tr><td>Wildlife reserves: species-focused protection with limited use</td></tr><tr><td>Conservation areas: biodiversity + sustainable development</td></tr><tr><td>Biodiversity hotspots: high endemism + high threat</td></tr><tr><td>Wetlands: water-saturated ecosystems with high biodiversity</td></tr><tr><td>Ramsar sites: internationally important wetlands</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ National parks and wildlife reserves are identical. ✅ National parks protect whole ecosystems; wildlife reserves focus on specific species.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ All wetlands are Ramsar sites. ✅ Only wetlands meeting international criteria are designated Ramsar sites.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Biodiversity hotspots are the same as protected areas. ✅ Hotspots are biogeographic regions; protected areas are legally designated zones.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Conservation areas prohibit all human activity. ✅ They integrate conservation with sustainable community use.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Protected areas (national parks, wildlife reserves, conservation areas) and internationally recognized zones (biodiversity hotspots, Ramsar wetlands) are key tools for in-situ conservation. Each type balances ecological protection with human use differently, and Nepal hosts examples of all categories.</p><hr><h2>Protected Areas, Biodiversity Hotspots and Endangered Species</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Detailed notes on 02 national parks wildlife reserves conservation areas biodiversity hotspots wetland and ramsar sites wildlife importance causes of extinction and conservation strategies iucn categories of threatened species endangered species in nepal within the conservation biology unit.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Protected areas: designated areas for conservation; in Nepal: 10 national parks, 6 conservation areas, 1 hunting reserve, and Ramsar wetlands.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>National parks: Chitwan (1973, Bengal tiger + one-horned rhino), Bardia, Banke, Shuklaphanta, Koshi Tappu, Parsa, Rara, Langtang (1976), Sagarmatha (1976), Annapurna (1986), Manaslu (1986), Makalu (1992), Kanchenjunga (1989). NEB commonly cites 10 national parks; several sources list 11–14 depending on inclusion of the newer parks.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Conservation areas (6): Kanchenjunga, Manaslu, Shey-Phoksundo, Khoratadi, Khaptad, and Barun.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Ramsar sites: Koshi Tappu, Rara, Chitwan (Terai wetlands) — internationally important wetlands.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>IUCN Red List categories: Extinct (EX), Extinct in the Wild (EW), Critically Endangered (CR), Endangered (EN), Vulnerable (VU), Near Threatened (NT), Least Concern (LC).</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Threatened species: CR, EN, VU (at risk of extinction); Near Threatened; Rare; Extinct.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Endangered species in Nepal: Bengal tiger (EN), one-horned rhino (VU), snow leopard (VU), Asian elephant (EN), goral, musk deer, rhesus macaque, red panda (VU), Himalayan tahr (NT), kingfisher (some CR).</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Causes of extinction: habitat loss, poaching, pollution, climate change, invasive species, overexploitation.</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Conservation strategies: protected areas, community forestry, anti-poaching laws (NTNC), ex-situ (zoos, seed banks), awareness, international agreements (CITES).</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>NEB focus: national parks, wildlife reserves, conservation areas, Ramsar sites, IUCN categories, endangered species in Nepal, causes of extinction, and conservation strategies.</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>Wildlife: importance (ecological services, economy, culture) and causes of extinction.</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>Biodiversity conservation involves safeguarding and scientifically managing diverse life forms to sustain their levels and provide lasting benefits for current and future generations.</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>It protects plant, animal, microbial, and genetic resources crucial for food production, agriculture, and ecosystem functions like soil fertilization, nutrient recycling, pest control, erosion prevention, and crop and tree pollination.</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>Conservation practices aim to manage nature and resources for present and future generations, ensuring the sustained well-being of humanity.</td></tr><tr><td>16</td><td>There are two basic strategies of biodiversity conservation: a.</td></tr><tr><td>17</td><td>In-Situ Conservation (within natural habitat): This method protects organisms in their natural habitats like national parks and other protected areas.</td></tr><tr><td>18</td><td>For instance, in Chitwan National Park, we have centers for Elephant Breeding, Gharial Conservation, and Vulture Conservation.</td></tr><tr><td>19</td><td>Ex-Situ Conservation (outside natural habitat): This type of conservation strategy includes protection of animals outside their natural habitats such as botanical or zoological gardens, cryopreservation, seed gene banks etc.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Nepal: 10 national parks, 6 conservation areas, 1 hunting reserve, Ramsar wetlands.</td></tr><tr><td>Chitwan (1973): first park; Bengal tiger + one-horned rhino; UNESCO World Heritage.</td></tr><tr><td>Sagarmatha (1976): Everest; UNESCO World Heritage.</td></tr><tr><td>IUCN: EX, EW, CR, EN, VU, NT, LC.</td></tr><tr><td>Endangered in Nepal: tiger (EN), rhino (VU), snow leopard (VU), elephant (EN), red panda (VU).</td></tr><tr><td>Causes of extinction: habitat loss, poaching, pollution, climate change, invasive species.</td></tr><tr><td>Strategies: in-situ, ex-situ, community forestry, anti-poaching, CITES, awareness.</td></tr><tr><td>Biodiversity conservation involves safeguarding and scientifically managing diverse life forms to sustain their levels and provide lasting benefits for current and future generations.</td></tr><tr><td>It protects plant, animal, microbial, and genetic resources crucial for food production, agriculture, and ecosystem functions like soil fertilization, nutrient recycling, pest control, erosion prevention, and crop and tree pollination.</td></tr><tr><td>Conservation practices aim to manage nature and resources for present and future generations, ensuring the sustained well-being of humanity.</td></tr><tr><td>There are two basic strategies of biodiversity conservation: a.</td></tr><tr><td>In-Situ Conservation (within natural habitat): This method protects organisms in their natural habitats like national parks and other protected areas.</td></tr><tr><td>• National parks</td></tr><tr><td>• Wildlife reserves</td></tr><tr><td>• Conservation areas</td></tr><tr><td>• Biodiversity hotspots</td></tr><tr><td>• Wetland</td></tr><tr><td>• Ramsar sites</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ Misconception about 02 national parks wildlife reserves conservation areas biodiversity hotspots wetland and ramsar sites wildlife importance causes of extinction and conservation strategies iucn categories of threatened species endangered species in nepal. ✅ Scientific explanation.</td></tr><tr><td>Chitwan National Park (1973) is the FIRST and most famous; it is home to the Bengal tiger and one-horned rhino.</td></tr><tr><td>Sagarmatha National Park (1976) includes Mount Everest; it is a UNESCO World Heritage Site.</td></tr><tr><td>IUCN "CR" = Critically Endangered (highest risk); "EN" = Endangered; "VU" = Vulnerable.</td></tr><tr><td>A national park is more strictly protected (no human habitation) than a conservation area (allows local people inside).</td></tr><tr><td>Ramsar sites are internationally important WETLANDS (not the same as national parks).</td></tr><tr><td>Community forests (in-situ) and national parks are BOTH conservation strategies; they differ in management (community vs government).</td></tr><tr><td>The one-horned rhinoceros (Rhinoceros unicornis) is VU (Vulnerable) globally; the Bengal tiger is EN (Endangered).</td></tr><tr><td>Distinguish between key concepts in Biodiversity and Conservation.</td></tr><tr><td>Understand the differences between similar topics.</td></tr><tr><td>Practice identifying the correct application of principles.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Summary of 02 national parks wildlife reserves conservation areas biodiversity hotspots wetland and ramsar sites wildlife importance causes of extinction and conservation strategies iucn categories of threatened species endangered species in nepal. Nepal protects its biodiversity through 10 national parks (Chitwan, Bardia, Banke, Shuklaphanta, Koshi Tappu, Parsa, Ranipurna, Sagarmatha, Langtang, Annapurna, Makalu), 6 conservation areas (Kanchenjunga, Manaslu, etc.), 1 hunting reserve, and Ramsar wetlands. Chitwan (1973) and Sagarmatha (1976) are UNESCO World Heritage Sites. The IUCN Red List (EX, EW, CR, EN, VU, NT, LC) tracks species risk; in Nepal, the Bengal tiger (EN), one-horned rhino (VU), snow leopard (VU), and Asian elephant (EN) are key endangered species. Threats include habitat loss, poaching, pollution, and climate change. Conservation strategies (in-situ, ex-situ, community forestry, anti-poaching, CITES) are essential to prevent extinction. NEB focuses on protected areas, IUCN categories, endangered species, causes of extinction, and conservation strategies. Biodiversity conservation involves safeguarding and scientifically managing diverse life forms
to sustain their levels and provide lasting benefits for current and future generations. It protects
plant,  animal,  microbial,  and genetic resources  crucial  for food production, agriculture,  and
ecosystem functions like soil fertilization, nutrient recycling,  pest control, erosion  prevention,
and crop and tree pollination.  Conservation practices aim  to manage nature and resources  for
pr</p><hr><h2>Protected Areas and Wildlife</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Protected areas: designated areas for conservation; in Nepal: 10 national parks, 6 conservation areas, 1 hunting reserve, and Ramsar wetlands.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>National parks: Chitwan (1973, Bengal tiger + one-horned rhino), Bardia, Banke, Shuklaphanta, Koshi Tappu, Parsa, Rara, Langtang (1976), Sagarmatha (1976), Annapurna (1986), Manaslu (1986), Makalu (1992), Kanchenjunga (1989). NEB commonly cites 10 national parks; several sources list 11–14 depending on inclusion of the newer parks.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Conservation areas (6): Kanchenjunga, Manaslu, Shey-Phoksundo, Khoratadi, Khaptad, and Barun.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Ramsar sites: Koshi Tappu, Rara, Chitwan (Terai wetlands) — internationally important wetlands.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>IUCN Red List categories: Extinct (EX), Extinct in the Wild (EW), Critically Endangered (CR), Endangered (EN), Vulnerable (VU), Near Threatened (NT), Least Concern (LC).</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Threatened species: CR, EN, VU (at risk of extinction); Near Threatened; Rare; Extinct.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Endangered species in Nepal: Bengal tiger (EN), one-horned rhino (VU), snow leopard (VU), Asian elephant (EN), goral, musk deer, rhesus macaque, red panda (VU), Himalayan tahr (NT), kingfisher (some CR).</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Causes of extinction: habitat loss, poaching, pollution, climate change, invasive species, overexploitation.</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Conservation strategies: protected areas, community forestry, anti-poaching laws (NTNC), ex-situ (zoos, seed banks), awareness, international agreements (CITES).</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>NEB focus: national parks, wildlife reserves, conservation areas, Ramsar sites, IUCN categories, endangered species in Nepal, causes of extinction, and conservation strategies.</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>Wildlife: importance (ecological services, economy, culture) and causes of extinction.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Nepal: 10 national parks, 6 conservation areas, 1 hunting reserve, Ramsar wetlands.</td></tr><tr><td>Chitwan (1973): first park; Bengal tiger + one-horned rhino; UNESCO World Heritage.</td></tr><tr><td>Sagarmatha (1976): Everest; UNESCO World Heritage.</td></tr><tr><td>IUCN: EX, EW, CR, EN, VU, NT, LC.</td></tr><tr><td>Endangered in Nepal: tiger (EN), rhino (VU), snow leopard (VU), elephant (EN), red panda (VU).</td></tr><tr><td>Causes of extinction: habitat loss, poaching, pollution, climate change, invasive species.</td></tr><tr><td>Strategies: in-situ, ex-situ, community forestry, anti-poaching, CITES, awareness.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>Chitwan National Park (1973) is the FIRST and most famous; it is home to the Bengal tiger and one-horned rhino.</td></tr><tr><td>Sagarmatha National Park (1976) includes Mount Everest; it is a UNESCO World Heritage Site.</td></tr><tr><td>IUCN "CR" = Critically Endangered (highest risk); "EN" = Endangered; "VU" = Vulnerable.</td></tr><tr><td>A national park is more strictly protected (no human habitation) than a conservation area (allows local people inside).</td></tr><tr><td>Ramsar sites are internationally important WETLANDS (not the same as national parks).</td></tr><tr><td>Community forests (in-situ) and national parks are BOTH conservation strategies; they differ in management (community vs government).</td></tr><tr><td>The one-horned rhinoceros (Rhinoceros unicornis) is VU (Vulnerable) globally; the Bengal tiger is EN (Endangered).</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Nepal protects its biodiversity through 10 national parks (Chitwan, Bardia, Banke, Shuklaphanta, Koshi Tappu, Parsa, Ranipurna, Sagarmatha, Langtang, Annapurna, Makalu), 6 conservation areas (Kanchenjunga, Manaslu, etc.), 1 hunting reserve, and Ramsar wetlands. Chitwan (1973) and Sagarmatha (1976) are UNESCO World Heritage Sites. The IUCN Red List (EX, EW, CR, EN, VU, NT, LC) tracks species risk; in Nepal, the Bengal tiger (EN), one-horned rhino (VU), snow leopard (VU), and Asian elephant (EN) are key endangered species. Threats include habitat loss, poaching, pollution, and climate change. Conservation strategies (in-situ, ex-situ, community forestry, anti-poaching, CITES) are essential to prevent extinction. NEB focuses on protected areas, IUCN categories, endangered species, causes of extinction, and conservation strategies.</p><hr><h2>Wildlife: Importance, Causes of Extinction and Conservation Strategies</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Wildlife includes all non-domesticated plants, animals, and microorganisms living in natural habitats, forming essential components of ecosystems.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Wildlife provides ecosystem services: pollination, seed dispersal, nutrient cycling, pest control, and maintenance of food webs.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Wildlife has economic value through ecotourism, medicinal resources, genetic diversity for agriculture, and sustainable harvest of timber and non-timber products.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Wildlife holds cultural, aesthetic, and ethical value, supporting indigenous knowledge and spiritual traditions.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Major causes of extinction: habitat destruction and fragmentation, overexploitation (hunting, poaching), invasive species, pollution, climate change, and disease.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Habitat loss is the leading cause of species extinction, driven by deforestation, agriculture, urbanization, and infrastructure development.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Overexploitation occurs when harvesting exceeds reproduction rates, as seen in ivory poaching and overfishing.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Invasive alien species outcompete native species for resources and alter ecosystem dynamics (e.g., Lantana camara in Nepal).</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Conservation strategies include legal protection (CITES, national laws), protected areas, captive breeding, habitat restoration, community-based conservation, and international cooperation.</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>CITES (Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species) regulates cross-border trade in threatened species through Appendix I, II, and III listings.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Wildlife = non-domesticated organisms in natural habitats</td></tr><tr><td>Ecosystem services: pollination, seed dispersal, nutrient cycling</td></tr><tr><td>Leading extinction cause: habitat destruction</td></tr><tr><td>Other causes: overexploitation, invasive species, pollution, climate change</td></tr><tr><td>Strategies: legal protection, protected areas, captive breeding, restoration</td></tr><tr><td>CITES regulates international wildlife trade</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ Extinction and endangerment are the same. ✅ Extinct means no living individuals remain; endangered means at high risk of extinction.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Poaching and hunting are identical. ✅ Hunting may be legal and regulated; poaching is illegal.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Invasive species always increase biodiversity. ✅ They usually reduce native biodiversity.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Conservation only means protecting animals. ✅ It includes plants, fungi, and ecosystems.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Wildlife underpins ecosystem function and human well-being, yet faces severe threats from habitat loss, overexploitation, invasive species, and climate change. Effective conservation combines legal frameworks, protected areas, captive breeding, and community engagement.</p><hr><h2>IUCN Categories of Threatened Species</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>The IUCN Red List is the world's most comprehensive inventory of the global conservation status of biological species, maintained by the International Union for Conservation of Nature.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Extinct (EX): no reasonable doubt that the last individual has died; no surveys can find it.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Extinct in the Wild (EW): survives only in captivity or cultivation, with no natural population.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Critically Endangered (CR): extremely high risk of extinction in the wild; e.g., population < 50 mature individuals or rapid decline.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Endangered (EN): very high risk of extinction in the wild; smaller populations or steeper declines than CR threshold.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Vulnerable (VU): high risk of extinction in the wild; larger populations than EN but still threatened.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Near Threatened (NT): close to qualifying for a threatened category.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Least Concern (LC): widespread and abundant; lowest risk of extinction.</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Data Deficient (DD): insufficient information to assess extinction risk.</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Not Evaluated (NE): not yet assessed against the criteria.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>IUCN Red List = global species conservation status inventory</td></tr><tr><td>EX: no living individuals remain</td></tr><tr><td>EW: survives only in captivity/cultivation</td></tr><tr><td>CR, EN, VU = threatened categories (in decreasing severity)</td></tr><tr><td>NT: close to threatened</td></tr><tr><td>LC: lowest risk</td></tr><tr><td>DD: insufficient data; NE: not evaluated</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Threatened' is a single category. ✅ 'Threatened' collectively refers to CR, EN, and VU.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Rare species are always endangered. ✅ Rarity alone doesn't imply endangerment; population trend matters.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Extinct in the wild means extinct. ✅ EW species still survive in captivity.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Data deficient means safe. ✅ DD means unknown risk, not low risk.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> The IUCN Red List uses standardized categories (EX, EW, CR, EN, VU, NT, LC, DD, NE) to assess extinction risk. Understanding these categories is essential for prioritizing conservation action and policy.</p><hr><h2>Endangered Species in Nepal</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Nepal's diverse geography (tropical to alpine) supports rich biodiversity, but many species are threatened by habitat loss, poaching, and human-wildlife conflict.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>One-horned rhinoceros (Rhinoceros unicornis) — Vulnerable; found in Chitwan and Bardia National Parks; recovered from ~100 individuals in 1960s to ~750 today.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Bengal tiger (Panthera tigris) — Endangered; population ~355 in Nepal (2022 census), concentrated in Terai protected areas.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Snow leopard (Panthera uncia) — Vulnerable; inhabits high Himalayas; threatened by poaching and retaliatory killing.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Red panda (Ailurus fulgens) — Endangered; found in eastern and central Himalayan forests; threatened by habitat fragmentation.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Gharial (Gavialis gangeticus) — Critically Endangered; freshwater crocodile of river systems; fewer than 200 in Nepal.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Himalayan musk deer (Moschus chrysogaster) — Endangered; hunted for musk pod; found in high-altitude forests.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Wild water buffalo (Bubalus arnee) — Endangered; restricted to Koshi Tappu Wildlife Reserve.</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Conservation efforts: protected areas, anti-poaching units, community forestry, transboundary cooperation (India, China), and captive breeding programs.</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Nepal's National Biodiversity Strategy and Action Plan (NBSAP) guides species conservation priorities.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Nepal hosts flagship endangered species: rhino, tiger, snow leopard, red panda</td></tr><tr><td>Rhino recovered from ~100 (1960s) to ~750</td></tr><tr><td>Tiger population ~355 (2022)</td></tr><tr><td>Gharial is Critically Endangered</td></tr><tr><td>Conservation: protected areas, anti-poaching, community forestry, transboundary cooperation</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ All endangered species live in national parks. ✅ Some live in community forests and buffer zones.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Tiger and leopard are the same species. ✅ They are distinct species with different habitats.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Red panda is a bear. ✅ It belongs to its own family Ailuridae, not Ursidae.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Gharial and mugger crocodile are identical. ✅ Gharial has a long narrow snout; mugger has a broad snout.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Nepal's endangered species — including rhino, tiger, snow leopard, red panda, and gharial — reflect both severe threats and successful recovery efforts. Protected areas, anti-poaching, community forestry, and international cooperation have driven notable conservation successes.</p><hr><h2>Comparison Tables</h2><h3>Concept Comparison</h3><table><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Description</th></tr><tr><td>1: Biodiversity and Conservation</td><td>Biodiversity is the variety of life at three levels — genetic, species, and ecosystem. Conservation biology protects it through in-situ strategies (protected areas, community forests, sacred groves) and ex-situ strategies (zoos, botanical gardens, seed banks, gene banks). Threats include habitat loss, overexploitation, pollution, invasive species, climate change, and poaching. Nepal, a Himalayan biodiversity hotspot with ~64% forest cover, leads globally in community forestry (22,000+ groups manage ~36% of forest land). NEB focuses on the concept of biodiversity, its conservation, and in-situ vs ex-situ strategies.</td></tr><tr><td>2: Conservation Biology â€” Biodiversity and Protected Areas</td><td>Biodiversity is the variety of life at three levels — genetic, species, and ecosystem. Conservation biology protects it through in-situ strategies (protected areas, community forests, sacred groves) and ex-situ strategies (zoos, botanical gardens, seed banks, gene banks). Threats include habitat loss, overexploitation, pollution, invasive species, climate change, and poaching. Nepal, a Himalayan biodiversity hotspot with ~64% forest cover, leads globally in community forestry (22,000+ groups manage ~36% of forest land). NEB focuses on the concept of biodiversity, its conservation, and in-situ vs ex-situ strategies. Biodiversity conservation involves safeguarding and scientifically managing diverse life forms
