pr</td></tr><tr><td>3: National Parks, Wildlife Reserves, Conservation Areas, Biodiversity Hotspots, Wetlands and Ramsar Sites</td><td>Protected areas (national parks, wildlife reserves, conservation areas) and internationally recognized zones (biodiversity hotspots, Ramsar wetlands) are key tools for in-situ conservation. Each type balances ecological protection with human use differently, and Nepal hosts examples of all categories.</td></tr><tr><td>4: Protected Areas, Biodiversity Hotspots and Endangered Species</td><td>Summary of 02 national parks wildlife reserves conservation areas biodiversity hotspots wetland and ramsar sites wildlife importance causes of extinction and conservation strategies iucn categories of threatened species endangered species in nepal. Nepal protects its biodiversity through 10 national parks (Chitwan, Bardia, Banke, Shuklaphanta, Koshi Tappu, Parsa, Ranipurna, Sagarmatha, Langtang, Annapurna, Makalu), 6 conservation areas (Kanchenjunga, Manaslu, etc.), 1 hunting reserve, and Ramsar wetlands. Chitwan (1973) and Sagarmatha (1976) are UNESCO World Heritage Sites. The IUCN Red List (EX, EW, CR, EN, VU, NT, LC) tracks species risk; in Nepal, the Bengal tiger (EN), one-horned rhino (VU), snow leopard (VU), and Asian elephant (EN) are key endangered species. Threats include habitat loss, poaching, pollution, and climate change. Conservation strategies (in-situ, ex-situ, community forestry, anti-poaching, CITES) are essential to prevent extinction. NEB focuses on protected areas, IUCN categories, endangered species, causes of extinction, and conservation strategies. Biodiversity conservation involves safeguarding and scientifically managing diverse life forms
